<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amorites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amorite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Amorites were a Semitic-speaking people of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, originally seen by Mesopotamian sources as nomadic outsiders. In the late third millennium BCE their migrations put pressure on the Akkadian and Neo-Sumerian empires. Amorite dynasties later ruled many Mesopotamian cities, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and an early Assyrian line under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amorites.jpg" alt="Amorites" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuneiform_Clay_Tablets_from_Amorite_Kingdom_of_Mari,_1st_Half_of_2nd_Mill._BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Amorites were a Northwest Semitic-speaking Bronze Age people who originated in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and first appear in Sumerian records around 2500 BCE. The most common view places their homeland in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, identified with Jebel Bishri or the Syrian steppe. In Sumerian and Akkadian texts the term Amurru, written mar.tu, refers to the Amorites, their principal deity and their kingdom. Tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> show them as a rural group on the middle and upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and for the Akkadian emperors mar.tu was one of the four quarters around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>. Naram-Sin of Akkad records defeating a coalition of Sumerian cities and Amorites near Jebel Bishri around 2240 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the last days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, immigrating Amorites were such a force that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-sin">Shu-Sin</a> built a wall of 270 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to hold them off. Mesopotamian texts describe them as nomadic tribes under chiefs, often with contempt. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> declined, Amorite rulers gained power in several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, a rise that peaked in the Old Babylonian period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a> of Ekallatum conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> became the major power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a> and his successors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, who destroyed the Amorite kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>.</p><p>An Amorite presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is thought to date from the 19th century BCE; the Fourteenth Dynasty had rulers with names such as Yakbim, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> seem to have included Amorites. The Amorite era in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> ended in the 16th century BCE with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> occupied around 1595 BCE. Syrian Amorites then came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian Empire</a>, and from about 1200 BCE they disappear from the record, displaced or absorbed by other West Semitic peoples, among whom the Arameans rose to prominence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Amorite language is known only from personal names and loanwords in other languages' texts, and was closely related to Canaanite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Their chief god was Amurru, with a consort Ašratum, whose name is cognate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>. Dagan was supreme in many cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> uses the name for highlanders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, descendants of Canaan in Genesis, and for the kings Sihon and Og east of the Jordan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amorites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ur-Nanshe, 'son of Gu-NI.DU', left many inscriptions, nearly all about building temples; one records a channel dug against the <b>Amorites</b>, and several say he had the ships of Dilmun bring timber as tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 3rd and early 2nd millennia BCE the same gods appear in the personal names of the <b>Amorites</b>, whose homeland lay in the middle Euphrates and lower Khabur regions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
